      "content": "Wait, \"highly significant correlation\"? I'm a bit worried about that. If a reviewer sees \"highly significant\" and then sees 0.06, they might think we're being imprecise or even trying to mislead them, which could kill our chances entirely.\n\nCan we push the language a bit more? Instead of calling it \"highly significant,\" maybe we can describe it as \"demonstrating a clear and definitive link\" or something that captures that momentum you mentioned? I want to make sure they don't just see it as a failed test. How would you write that section to make the evidence feel undeniable?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
